=====================================
2️⃣  INTRODUCTION TO COMPUTER
=====================================

What is a computer?
An electronic machine that takes in data, processes it,
and produces results — fast and accurately.

HOW IT WORKS: Input → Process → Output
  - Input:   keyboard, mouse, mic, camera
  - Process: CPU calculates
  - Output:  screen, speakers, printer

HARDWARE vs. SOFTWARE
  Hardware = physical parts (stays the same until changed)
  Software = instructions (easy to add/update/remove)

KEY HARDWARE COMPONENTS
  CPU       — "Brain"; has Control Unit + ALU
  RAM       — Fast, short-term; cleared when powered off
  ROM       — Permanent; stores startup instructions
  Storage   — Long-term home for files and programs

STORAGE TYPES
  HDD → SSD → USB/Cards → Cloud

INPUT DEVICES:  keyboard, mouse, mic, scanner, webcam
OUTPUT DEVICES: monitor, printer, speakers, projector
  * Touchscreen = both input AND output

BINARY
  Computers only understand 1s and 0s.
  Bit → Byte (8 bits) → KB → MB → GB

SOFTWARE TYPES
  System software      — OS (Windows, macOS, Linux, Android, iOS)
  Application software — browsers, word processors, games, messaging

NETWORKING
  Linking 2+ computers to share data.
  LAN = Local Area Network (home/office)


=====================================
3️⃣  COMPUTER HARDWARE & NETWORKING ESSENTIALS
=====================================

CORE INTERNAL COMPONENTS
  Motherboard   — Main circuit board linking everything
  CPU           — Executes instructions
  RAM           — Temporary storage for active tasks
  GPU           — Renders graphics
  Power supply  — Converts wall power for components

PERIPHERALS
  Input:  keyboard, mouse, scanner, mic, webcam, touchscreen
  Output: monitor, printer, speakers, projector

STORAGE COMPARISON
  HDD        — High capacity, low cost, spinning disks
  SSD        — Faster, silent, no moving parts
  USB flash  — Small, portable
  Cloud      — Remote servers, internet access

NETWORK HARDWARE
  Router       — Shares internet; directs traffic
  Switch       — Connects multiple wired devices
  Modem        — Bridges network to ISP
  Access point — Adds Wi-Fi
  Firewall     — Filters traffic; protects network
  NIC          — Lets a device join a network

DATA FLOW
  Internet → Modem → Router → Switch → Devices

CABLE TYPES
  Ethernet · Fiber optic · Coaxial · Wi-Fi · Bluetooth


